From 010d3aa022af4acdb377cd07a52a10c1e62109cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 02:56:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 65/71] inline-3d(Phase 2): occlusion on by default, delete the
 exclusion machinery
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0064 shipped the draw-order plane split behind --inline-3d-occlusion and
left the Phase-1 declared-exclusion mechanism dormant beside it, so the hardware
A/B was a relaunch rather than a rebuild. That A/B passed: per-pixel occlusion of
UNDECLARED content confirmed live, the DP overlay atlas confirmed dormant,
translucent chrome over the weave verified visually. So the split becomes the
product default and the mechanism it replaces goes away.

  * chrome_main_delegate.cc appends --inline-3d-occlusion BY DEFAULT, alongside
    enable-inline-3d and inline-3d-sync-weave. --disable-inline-3d-occlusion
    still wins over an explicit --inline-3d-occlusion and remains the escape
    hatch. ChromeContentBrowserClient forwards whatever survives to the children.

  * Blink: XRDisplayLayer gains a STATIC readonly `occlusionByDrawOrder`
    attribute — the capability the inline-3D SDK probes
    (`typeof XRDisplayLayer.occlusionByDrawOrder === 'boolean'`). Static because
    it mirrors a process-wide switch, is readable before any layer exists, and
    installing it on the interface object avoids the illegal-invocation trap a
    prototype getter read would hit. It reads the RENDERER's own command line,
    which is the same switch the GPU process's split runs under.

    excludeElement()/unexcludeElement() become validate-and-no-op: arguments are
    still checked, composite:"under" still throws NotSupportedError (2D UNDER the
    weave is a different pipeline and still unimplemented), and one throttled
    console warning per layer points the author at occlusionByDrawOrder. Nothing
    is recorded — there is no consumer left, so collecting the set would lie.

  * Everything the declared model needed is gone, end to end: the
    kInline3dOverlay tracked-element feature; Inline3dExclusion +
    inline_3d_exclusion_rects + inline_3d_exclusion_fixed_rects on the frame
    metadata, its mojom struct/fields/typemaps/traits/test, and the cc plumbing
    behind them (commit_state, layer_tree_host, layer_tree_impl,
    layer_tree_host_impl, layer_context and its three implementations,
    frame_widget, web_frame_widget_impl); the aggregator's exclusion merge (both
    CollectTrackedElementRects passes), the exclusion->quad join with its
    ID_JOIN_MISS / GEOM_FALLBACK / PROMOTION_LOST / UNCOMPOSITABLE / EXCL_ABSENT
    markers and per-id throttle, the overlay z-sort and the quad_ordinal that
    existed only to feed it; AggregatedFrame::inline_3d_overlays; display.cc's
    overlay resolve and overlay-resource suppression; and in the GPU stage the
    Windows overlay atlas, both mac atlases, their four members and the mac
    legacy overlay draw-back loop.

    Two things are kept deliberately. `best_match` stays: the canvas ID-MATCH
    FALLBACK still calls it, so it is a live dependency, not exclusion residue.
    The provider's SetBatchOverlay/SetBatchOverlayMac virtuals stay caller-less
    with a comment saying so — that is the channel for handing the DP an
    over-plane to composite itself (Phase 3), the runtime-side v4 contract is
    shipped, and keeping the binding costs nothing.

    The overlay half of SetInline3DRects goes with them: display.cc no longer
    resolves overlay mailboxes and nothing consumed them, so the parameter and
    inline_3d_overlays_ would have been a hollow duct.

  * docs/integration-points.md §1-3 describe the draw-order model instead of the
    exclusion model, including the list of content that cannot be lifted and is
    therefore still woven: backdrop-filter and all render-pass quads
    (pixel-moving filters), non-kSrcOver blend modes, 3D sorting contexts,
    protected video.

WHAT THE KILL SWITCH NOW MEANS. After this patch --disable-inline-3d-occlusion
disables the plane split AND leaves no atlas, because the atlas is deleted: the
flag-off path is neither Phase 2 nor Phase 1, it is "no occlusion at all", and
undeclared 2D over a tile is woven again. That is accepted and intended. The
switch guards against plane-split BUGS — a bad split is worse than no split —
not against needing a full Phase-1 revert; reverting Phase 1 means reverting this
patch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
